ity bill Watson a professor of  Montreal's McGill University is critical  of some of the tactics of the student  movement people were gathering in their  thousands  and then marching and more or less  random order on different routes every  night they've gathered in support from  all sorts of people with all sorts of  grievances people from the Occupy  movement people from the  anti-globalization movement people from  a more traditional labor union posture  even though it's in a sense a local  fight against tuition hike it's also a  broader fight against the privatization  of public services and against austerity  like measures the anarcho Panda is the  alter ego of a junior college philosophy  professor who's taken an  national peacekeeping role in the  student strike PNR cope and spends a lot  of his I assume paid time because he's  not given his classes his classes have  been suspended but his pay has not been  suspended trying to bring police and  opponents of the protests and the  protesters into a better state of mind  it's the kind of symbol that truly  disturbs the police officers who are  used to being very rough to the  protesters and when you see this huge  panda in front of you it's kind of  disturbing because he's there with his  big smile wanting to hug all the police  the students were victims of violence  from the police fairly early on as a  teacher and somebody that cares for  students I started looking for ways to  try to help the students with that when  the right police shows up I try to be  there in the front line and to interact  with them they have a very strict  behavioral protocol they're not allowed  to be human essentially they're not  allowed to talk to you they're not  allowed to look at you in the eye  they're just there to be the sort of  brute force of the system by talking to  them and by interacting with them I try  to remind them that they are people too  and that they will be judged both  legally and by their own conscience  but not all demonstrators were as  pacifistic as the anarcho panda  incidents like the smoke bomb attack in  the underground subway system and the  vandalization of the montreal economic  Institute's downtown offices marred the  movements preferred image of nonviolent  civil disobedience as protests grew  larger and more disruptive and as  conflicts with police grew more violent  the government of Quebec passed bill 78  an emergency law which suspended the  university term essentially locking out  the strikers it also banned masks during  protests introduced huge fines on  protest organizers and demanded prior  notice of protest routes you've  essentially got traffic block in  unpredictable ways every night and  you're running a city other people are  trying to get on with their lives it's  not unreasonable to require protests of  more than 50 people to tell the police 8  hours in advance that's all and those  you have to give look we're gonna gather  here and we're gonna go from here to  there and for us it's a it's a totally  unacceptable measures now the police  officers will be able to say that you  can protest but not there and you have  to go in the field and we want to put  this in downtown no you will protest in  the neighborhood away from everyone  where you are seen by no one  and  you disturb nothing the idea was to  disrupt it was very effective in  disrupting but I think as civilized  society has the right to regulate this  kind of thing and say look people who  are not interested in this issue who  don't in fact agree with you do have the  right to conduct their lives in the  normal way that they see fit but in the  democratic society you should be able to  protest where you want and to express  yourself where you want and when you  want and that's a lot more important  than any minor economic disturbance of a  few hours in any downtown Canadian  Constitution a lawyer Julius gray a  supporter of the student protests  explains the severe civil liabilities of  bill 78 there could be a class action by  some person that comes in and says I'm a  student I wanted to go to my classes and  I lost $3,000 and if there's 10,000 of  them civil liability could ruin a  student for the rest of his life  anyone who lost the entry of a education  institution can receive a fine of about  $10,000 per person if you are a local  Student Association you can go up to  $50,000 and if you are a national for  union representative like me you can  have up to a hundred and fifty thousand  dollars per day there was some  justification in order to increase the  amount of those fines because incentive  matters and if you have a particular  action which is blocking access to  colleges blocking bridges so that people  cannot move freely violating the  freedoms of other people and this  particular course of action is costing  you $1,500  you're not gonna react the same way as  if it's costing you a quarter of a  million this law will apply only for one  year and we are actually in front of the  court to make this law declared  unconstitutional the Association of  lawyers association of judges everyone  I'm pretty sure we will win but it's  gonna be so long and when we will win  the law will no longer be applied and  the government will have succeeded in  going over the institutions that are  here protect civil liberties in the  civil right it is probably a law that  was not necessary and so in that sense  it's maybe a case of government  over regulation the argument against  bill 78 is that most of the powers that  one talks about already in the criminal  code I don't see any reason to pass a  law that forbids riots I mean riots are  prohibited in the Criminal Code of  Canada I don't see any advantage on the  other hand the civil liability the  delegation of powers and the Prevention  of spontaneous demonstrations are  serious serious invasions of civil  liberties the 78 was a panic move to  kill the mobilization but in fact it's  at the exact opposite that effect since  bill 78 we have seen hundreds of  thousands of citizens students citizens  each night going out with their pots and  pans banging on them to show their  disapproval this bill is a direct threat  to certain rights that all citizens have  not only students to express their  opinion and to freely assemble Quebec  universities are scheduled to reopen for  class in August and a Supreme Court  decision on the constitutionality of  bill 78 is likely several months away  though the nightly protests have  continued in much smaller numbers the  student unions are planning another  massive protest for August 22nd where  hundreds of thousands are expected to  march through Montreal it remains  difficult to judge bill 78 as a  reasonable intervention for the public  good or a draconian law imposed by a  panicking government mass movements like  the Tea Party the Arab Spring and the  Occupy movement suggest a new paradigm  of civil disobedience in the global  zeitgeist but the conflict in Quebec  presents a dilemma that has yet to be  solved where is the line between freely  expressing dissent and depriving other  people of their liberties
 

